  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 208 PM EDT Wed Aug 12 2026\n\nRest of Today-Friday...Isolated showers and storms have begun to\ndevelop across the area this afternoon, with a somewhat delayed\nstart to the sea breeze. The sea breeze will be the main focus for\nconvection today, though CAMs suggest outflow from ongoing storms\nacross north Florida sparking additional activity generally north\nof Orlando. PoPs reflect this possibility, though PoPs overall \nremain below normal for mid-August (up to 30-40%). Slower storm\nmotions could support outflow collisions into this evening, but\nconvection is forecast to diminish by around 10 PM or so. The 15Z\nXMR sounding found DCAPE near 1000 J/kg, so will need to watch for\na few strong storms this afternoon with gusts to 50 mph. Slow\nmoving storms and ample moisture could also lead to a quick 1-3\"\nin a few spots.\n\nExpect similar conditions for the remainder of the work week, as\nhigh pressure dominates the local pattern. Light W/SW flow backs\nS/SE at the coast and increases to around 10 mph with the\ndevelopment of the sea breeze in the afternoons. PWATs 2-2.3\" and\nthe sea breeze collision over the eastern half of the peninsula \nwill continue to support scattered showers and storms, but PoPs \nare still below normal (30-40%). Strong storms with gusty winds to\n50 mph and locally heavy rainfall will remain threats, as drier\nair aloft due to the Saharan Air Layer (SAL) lingers. Hot\ntemperatures in the 90s and muggy dew points will continue to\nproduce heat indices near Heat Advisory criteria (108F).\nAdditional Heat Advisories are possible each day. Regardless, be\nsure to practice heat safety!\n\nSaturday-Wednesday...The Atlantic ridge axis builds back towards\nthe peninsula on Saturday. Then, surface ridging once again moves\nwestward across the peninsula and into the Gulf through early next\nweek. Aloft, a strong upper level high moves southward into the\nSoutheast US and flattens through the period. All of this results\nin little perceptible change to the overall pattern for east\ncentral Florida. PoPs remain below normal, generally 30-40% or\nless, though most areas will likely remain dry most days. Sunday\nlooks to be the driest day, with PoPs below 20% area-wide. Strong\nstorms will remain possible each afternoon, with waves of SAL \nsupporting wind gusts to 50 mph in storms that can survive the \noverall subsidence. Any areas that see rainfall could pick up a \nquick 1-3\" due to light storm motions and PWATs 2+\" at times. The \nafternoon sea breeze will have to work to overcome the light \noffshore flow. Highs in the mid-90s will creep towards the upper \n90s at times, with humid conditions continuing. Additional Heat \nAdvisories will need to be considered through at least the \nweekend.",
